Powerup Simulator
=================

A C++17/OpenGL learning project. Milestone 2 renders a blue cube on an 8x8
world-unit ground plane with a perspective orbit camera and depth testing.
GLFW creates the window; bundled GLAD 1 loads OpenGL. The context requests
OpenGL 3.3 core and the shaders use GLSL 330. Face colors suggest shape;
there is no lighting system yet.

Build and run (from the repository root)
----------------------------------------

Requires CMake, a C/C++ compiler, GLFW development files, OpenGL development
files, and a working graphical session. No new external dependencies.

    cmake -S . -B build
    cmake --build build -j2
    ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure
    ./build/sim

Configuration generates build instructions; compilation builds sim and the
small scene math test. The existing build directory uses Unix Makefiles;
keep its generator when reconfiguring. Shader sources live in src/main.cpp,
so shader edits require rebuilding. Startup prints the vendor, renderer,
OpenGL version, and controls. Compilation/link failures print shader logs
and exit with status 1. No graphics environment override is required by
our successful checks; see PROGRESS.md for WSL startup observations.

Controls and manual checks
--------------------------

- Left/right arrows: orbit horizontally. Up/down: change elevation.
- + or =: zoom in. -: zoom out. Keypad +/- also work.
- R: reset camera. Escape or window close button: exit.
- The window must have keyboard focus. Hold keys for continuous movement.
- Orbit around the cube and confirm nearer faces hide farther faces.
  The ground is drawn after the cube; depth testing keeps it behind the cube.
- Zoom to both limits (2.5 and 15 world units from the target). Elevation
  stays between 0.1 and 1.45 radians, keeping the camera above the ground.
- Resize to a tall and then a wide window: the cube should keep its
  proportions. Minimize/restore and confirm rendering resumes normally.

How the stage works
-------------------

The VBO stores 36 cube vertices followed by 6 ground vertices. Each vertex
has six floats: x, y, z, red, green, blue. The VAO records a six-float stride
and a three-float color offset, both expressed in bytes. One shader program
and one VAO/VBO serve both objects, with two draw calls per frame.

The unit cube starts centered at the origin in object coordinates. Its
model matrix translates it up by 0.5 so its bottom rests at world y = 0.
The ground uses an identity model transform. The view matrix places the
world relative to a camera looking at (0, 0.5, 0). The projection matrix
uses a 45-degree vertical field of view and the current framebuffer aspect
ratio, with near/far clipping distances of 0.1 and 50 world units.

In the vertex shader, projection * view * model * position applies these
transforms from right to left. src/scene_math.h contains only the vector
and matrix operations needed here, using column-major storage and a
right-handed world with +Y up. Its CPU-only tests check translation, view
orientation, near/far depth mapping, and aspect correction.

Each frame processes events, moves the camera using elapsed seconds,
clears color and depth, uploads transforms, and draws. Camera motion caps
elapsed time at 0.1 seconds to discard a large jump after a stall. If either
framebuffer dimension is zero, projection and drawing are skipped while
event handling continues. GPU resources are deleted before the context.

Exercise (10-20 minutes)
-----------------------

Make the cube hover by changing cubeModel's y translation from 0.5f to:

    0.75f + 0.25f * std::sin(static_cast<float>(currentTime) * 2.0f)

Predict its minimum/maximum bottom height and its period before rebuilding.
The constant 2.0 is angular speed in radians per second. Keep the ground
and camera target fixed while trying the change.

1. Why does hovering belong in the cube's model matrix instead of the view?
2. Why must the projection use framebuffer width/height, and why must the
   depth buffer be cleared every frame?
